      <h1 class="narrative-title">Steep Bluffs, Landslides, Floodplains & Coal Mines</h1>
      <p class="narrative-lede">
        Navigating critical areas across King and Snohomish counties: coastal bluff drainage management, FEMA floodplain insurance rules, Elevation Certificates, and East King County historical coal mine overlays.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Real Estate Coach Callout -->
    <div class="coach-callout">
      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        The stunning terrain of Western Washington comes with distinct geological considerations. Here is my rule of thumb for slope hazards: <strong>water is the main enemy</strong>. Landslide risks on Puget Sound bluffs or steep ravines are almost always triggered by uncontrolled surface runoff (like leaking gutters, misdirected downspouts, or removing deep-rooted native trees). If you are considering a home near a steep slope or in a river valley, check official GIS mapping early. For river valley properties, obtaining an official Elevation Certificate can often cut annual FEMA flood insurance premiums dramatically.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>Steep Slopes, Coastal Bluffs & Critical Area (ECA) Rules</h2>
      <p>
        Properties along Puget Sound coastal bluffs (such as Edmonds, Mukilteo, Shoreline, and West Seattle) or inland ravine slopes carry special local zoning designations called <strong>Environmentally Critical Areas (ECA)</strong> or Geologically Hazardous Areas:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Setback Buffers:</strong> Municipal codes generally require a 15 to 50-foot building setback from the top or toe of any slope exceeding 40% grade to preserve structural integrity.</li>
        <li><strong>Vegetation Management:</strong> Tree roots act as natural soil rebar. Cutting down mature trees on a bluff face or slope can destabilize topsoil layers and increase slide potential during wet winter months.</li>
        <li><strong>Stormwater Management:</strong> All roof runoff, driveway drainage, and sump pump discharges must be piped securely to approved tightlines at the bottom of the slope or municipal storm systems.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>River Floodplains: FEMA Zones & Elevation Certificates</h2>
      <p>
        Major river basins across Snohomish and King counties (including the Snohomish, Stillaguamish, Snoqualmie, and Skykomish rivers) contain mapped 100-year floodplains (FEMA Zones A and AE).
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Flood Risk Metric</th>
            <th>Conventional Loan Impact</th>
            <th>Homeowner Mitigation Strategy</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>FEMA Zone A / AE (100-Yr)</strong></td>
            <td>Mandatory flood insurance required by lenders.</td>
            <td>Obtain a licensed surveyor Elevation Certificate to verify structure height above Base Flood Elevation (BFE).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Crawl Space Construction</strong></td>
            <td>Underwriting review for hydrostatic pressure.</td>
            <td>Install engineered flood vents (1 sq in of vent per 1 sq ft of enclosed crawl space area).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>LOMA (Letter of Map Amendment)</strong></td>
            <td>Removes mandatory insurance requirement.</td>
            <td>File with FEMA if natural ground under the living structure sits higher than the surrounding flood plane.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Historical Coal Mine Overlay Zones (East King County)</h2>
      <p>
        During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, extensive underground coal mining took place in East King County communities including <strong>Newcastle, Issaquah, Renton, and Coal Creek</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        King County tracks these historical shaft networks using Coal Mine Hazard Overlay Maps. While most deep shafts were sealed decades ago, properties within mine hazard zones trigger mandatory seller disclosures on Form 17. Buyers should verify whether a home sits over shallow mine workings, unsealed air shafts, or deep bedrock formations before proceeding.
      </p>
    </section>

